module gunbc.jurisdiction_drop_accounting

import std.types { Bool, List }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }
import product.fabric.provider_route { JurisdictionDropEnrollment, CompliancePostureRevision }
import gunbc.rung_drop.roster { standing_rung_drops }

// THE ACCOUNTING JOIN FOR A JURISDICTION DROP ENROLLMENT, AND WHY IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN IN THE
// FABRIC. product.fabric.provider_route JurisdictionDropEnrollment carries a drop reference so that
// an exception suppressing TransferOrLocationUnresolved names the roster row accounting for it.
// Requiring the field means an enrollment with NO drop row has no constructor, and because a permit
// records the whole posture the row is reachable from an admitted permit -- but until this module
// existed NOTHING READ the reference, so the suppression executed while its accounting did not.
// Review 64697 named that correctly: an enrollment's entire effect is that a refusal does not fire,
// and a suppression whose accounting nothing reads is DESIGN section 5's escape hatch in its exact
// form -- a toggle whose only effect is "proceed as if the refusal had not fired".
//
// THE LAYER IS THE POINT. product.fabric.provider_route states its own authority direction: it holds
// provider-side route facts, and "the join against posture and use scope lives one layer up",
// because whether we are presently willing to use those facts is a fact about US. Our rung-drop
// roster is the same kind of fact, so the generic fabric must not import it any more than it
// enumerates jurisdictions -- a fabric that read gunbc's drop roster would be the inversion that
// module exists to refuse. The enrollment type stays there; the join to our accounting is here.
//
// THE JOIN KEY IS THE ROW'S OWN DECLARED IDENTITY. A RungDrop carries identity: NonEmptyStr and no
// module path, so the comparison is the enrollment's cited decl_name against that identity. The
// namespace authority already establishes that the reference RESOLVES to a real declaration; what
// this adds is that the declaration it names is a rostered drop that is still Standing. WHAT IT DOES
// NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly rather than left for a reader to discover: the module path is not
// compared, so a declaration elsewhere sharing a roster row's name would satisfy this join. That is
// the same reference-versus-name weakness as the population half below and is covered by the same
// trigger.
type EnrollmentAccountingStanding
  = EnrollmentAccounted { drop: DeclarationRef }
  | EnrollmentDropUnrostered { drop: DeclarationRef }

// ONE MATCH IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE BECAUSE THE SUBJECT IS A LOOKUP, NOT A COPRODUCT, so the
// discipline the fabric module keeps for coproducts is kept in the shape that does apply: the
// result carries the offending reference rather than a Bool, so a caller that must report the
// failure never walks the value a second time to find out which enrollment it was.
//
// Retired and absent collapse into ONE arm deliberately, and the collapse is not the
// Absent-versus-Unread conflation this corpus refuses elsewhere. Both mean the same thing to a
// consumer -- there is no STANDING accounting for this suppression -- and both carry the same
// remedy: declare one, or stop suppressing. standing_rung_drops is the roster's own filter, so a
// row retiring makes every enrollment citing it unaccounted with no edit here, which is the
// behaviour a retired drop must have.
//
// THERE IS NO ROSTER PARAMETER, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. The
// first spelling took one and then called standing_rung_drops() inside the fold, so a caller could
// pass a roster that was silently discarded -- review 64716 caught it, and it is the fabricated
// answer DESIGN section 5 forbids: the argument named the join input and did not supply it. Passing
// it through would have fixed that and left a worse hazard standing, because the parameter's
// admitting value is the FULL roster: a caller handing over rung_drop_roster rather than
// standing_rung_drops() would account for a RETIRED drop, which is this gate's whole subject. A
// parameter with a plausible wrong value that fails open is validation where construction was
// available. Reading the standing roster directly removes the argument and the hazard together:
// there is one authority for "which drops are still standing", it is gunbc.rung_drop.roster's own
// filter, and no call site can disagree with it.
fn enrollment_accounting(
  enrollment: JurisdictionDropEnrollment,
) -> EnrollmentAccountingStanding {
  if count(filter(standing_rung_drops(), d => d.identity == enrollment.drop.decl_name)) > 0 {
    EnrollmentAccounted { drop: enrollment.drop }
  } else {
    EnrollmentDropUnrostered { drop: enrollment.drop }
  }
}

// EVERY UNACCOUNTED ENROLLMENT, NOT THE FIRST. A posture is a small authored row and a reader fixing
// it wants the whole set, unlike a route admission where reporting one obligation at a time stops a
// caller shopping for the cheapest. An empty result reads as "none unaccounted" and needs no seed.
fn unaccounted_enrollments(
  enrollments: List<JurisdictionDropEnrollment>,
) -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  fold(
    enrollments,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, e) {
      match enrollment_accounting(enrollment: e) {
        EnrollmentAccounted { drop } => acc
        EnrollmentDropUnrostered { drop } => concat(acc, [drop])
      }
    },
  )
}

// THE POSTURE-LEVEL QUESTION, which is what a gate asks: does every suppression this posture carries
// name a standing accounting row? An empty list is the admitting answer.
fn posture_unaccounted_enrollments(posture: CompliancePostureRevision) -> List<DeclarationRef> {
  unaccounted_enrollments(enrollments: posture.jurisdiction_drop_enrollments)
}

// WHICH OF THE THREE HOLES THIS CLOSES, STATED SO A GREEN IS NOT READ AS MORE THAN IT IS. Review
// 64697 named three ways an enrollment could stand a wall down silently. Closed here: a drop
// reference naming no rostered row at all, and one naming a row that has been RETIRED. Open: an
// enrollment citing a real, standing row whose POPULATION describes some other path.
//
// THE THIRD IS BLOCKED RATHER THAN DECLINED. gunbc.rung_drop RungDropDeclaration carries
// population: List<String> -- prose. Checking that prose "names" a DeclarationRef means
// substring-matching a declaration name against a sentence, which is agreement by spelling between
// producer and consumer: exactly what product.fabric.provider_route argues against at length in its
// own opening, and it would read as coverage while enforcing nothing, which is worse than leaving
// the gap visible.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY SO IT CANNOT BE RETIRED WHILE THE GAP STAYS OPEN:
// gunbc.rung_drop RungDropDeclaration carrying its enrolled subjects as DECLARATION REFERENCES
// rather than prose -- sufficient for an enrollment to be checked against the drop row's actual
// population, and sufficient for this module's join to compare references rather than a name. A
// population sentence mentioning a path does NOT discharge it: that is the artifact, not the
// capability, and it would leave the next enrollment in the same position.
